 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Bounded 3D Distance Texture Ray Marching](1115-bounded-3d-distance-texture-ray-marching.md)
-Bounded 3D Distance Texture Ray Marching is a technique for rendering implicit surfaces defined by a 3D distance texture, specifically addressing the problem of invalid distance values at the texture boundaries. It employs two nested bounding boxes (an inner "red" box and an outer "green" box) to distinguish between valid texture data and a boundary shell where samples may be unreliable. Unlike standard texture lookups that might return erroneous distances near edges, this method returns the distance to the outer boundary for points in the invalid shell, ensuring the ray marcher steps safely without tunneling into undefined data. It enables the inclusion of pre-computed distance textures (e.g., the Stanford Bunny) into scenes using standard SDF composition operators. See [Bounded 3D Distance Texture Ray Marching](1115-bounded-3d-distance-texture-ray-marching.md).
